{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786482326,"nonce":"sQ1IzSA8eAfRLLAAZ1ZYSlzfrbI=","message":"RCA:\n\nThe stripping is a side effect of dccab10473b5 (\"trailers: reject\nmalformed Fixes: trailer values\", 2026-06-19), which added an\nunconditional check requiring Fixes: values to start with 6+ lowercase\nhex characters (a git short-hash), rejecting anything else.\n\nThat commit was itself a fix for a real bug: Jani Nikula reported that\na reviewer's rhetorical \"Fixes: ?\" in a reply email was being picked\nup by b4 shazam and applied literally as a trailer. The fix (and its\ntest suite) was deliberately generalized beyond that one case -- it\nalso rejects plain prose like \"Fixes: some description with no hash\",\nguarding against any line starting with \"Fixes:\" in follow-up\ncommentary being misparsed as a real trailer.\n\nSo the hash-only allowlist is intentional, not an oversight -- but it's\ntoo narrow. QEMU and similar projects use Fixes: to reference CVE IDs\nand issue/bug-tracker URLs, not just commit hashes, and those are\ncurrently being silently dropped.\n\nAny fix needs to keep rejecting arbitrary prose (the original bug)\nwhile accepting CVE-ID and URL forms, e.g. via an allowlist of\nspecific shapes (hash, CVE-\\d{4}-\\d+, https?://) rather than a\nblanket loosening of the check. Holding off on implementation until\nMarc-André confirms the exact conventions QEMU (and any other\naffected projects) expect.","files":null}]}